course: dictionary
lesson: 3218
title: Enrichment: making things richer
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Enrichment means making something better, richer or more interesting by adding to it. «In a zoo the food comes easily, so keepers have to give back the busy part of life, and that is called enrichment.» The keepers add something to the animal's life, so it becomes busy and interested again.
explain: Schools use the word too. «Enrichment week» means extra activities beyond normal lessons, such as cooking, chess, sport, clubs and trips. Enrichment activities and an enrichment programme add to what you learn in class.
explain: To spot enrichment, ask: has something been added to make life better or more interesting? If yes, it is enrichment. Adding vitamins to food is enrichment too, because it adds something useful.
fact: Enrichment is uncountable, so you say some enrichment, not two enrichments.
fact: In zoos, enrichment means toys, puzzles and changes to an animal's home to keep it busy and happy.
fact: «Food enrichment hides food in boxes, logs, ice blocks or puzzle feeders, so a bear uses its nose, claws and strength as in the wild.»
fact: «Scent enrichment brings new smells such as spices, perfumes or the dung of other animals; lions given zebra dung roll in it and sniff it for a long time.»
fact: Zoos check whether enrichment works by counting behaviour such as pacing (walking the same path again and again, often a sign of boredom or stress), play and resting before and after.
exercise: In a school, «enrichment clubs» offer cooking, chess or sport. What are enrichment clubs? | *Extra activities beyond normal lessons | Lessons that are exactly the same as maths | Rooms that nobody is allowed to enter | Clubs for sleeping during lessons
exercise: «New climbing frames, ropes, branches and hiding places are enrichment too.» What do these have in common? | They take away the animal's home | They are all food for the animals | They are only used for cleaning the cage | *They add something new to make an animal's home more interesting
exercise: Which of these is an example of enrichment in science? | Throwing away food that is still good | Breaking glass into small pieces | *Adding vitamins to food to make it more useful | Leaving a metal untouched in a box
test: What does enrichment mean? | Taking something away from it | Hiding something from view | *Making something better by adding to it | Making something quieter
test: Which is a good example of zoo enrichment? | Closing the animal's home for the day | Leaving the cage empty and dark | *Frozen fruit hidden in an ice block for a bear | Feeding the animals in complete silence
exam: Priya says «enrichment week» is just a normal lesson. Is she right? | No, it means a week with no school | *No, it means extra activities beyond normal lessons | Yes, because it has no activities at all | Yes, it is exactly the same as maths
